  "query_preview": "..A cold front is marching through northern Utah this\nevening, associated with a shortwave trough grazing the area. \nThis front is not expected to push much farther south than the \ncentral Wasatch Front before weakening later tonight. Showers with\nisolated embedded thunderstorms are accompanying this front. \nGiven the fast-moving nature of the parent trough, expect \nprecipitation to be short-lived, tapering off for the most part \nshortly after midnight. Expect generally light amounts of \nprecipitation with a few tenths of an inch at best of snow in the \nhigher elevations of northern Utah. \n\nAfter a brief period of zonal flow tonight, the flow will turn\nmore southwesterly again for Tuesday as the next shortwave trough\nswings towards the Great Basin. This trough will eject across\nnorthern Utah Tuesday night, gradually weakening as it does so.\nThis is expected to bring another quick round of light valley rain\nand mountain snow to northern Utah and southwest Wyoming Tuesday \nnight.\n\nAttention will then turn to the weekend storm system which could\nbring more widespread precipitation to the area. During the second\nhalf of the week, a closed low will slowly move into the PacNW.\nAhead of it, Utah will see a return to a southwesterly flow aloft.\nOn Friday, a weak shortwave disturbance rotating around the low \nis progged to lift across Utah, bringing an initial round of \nprecipitation to the area. Unsettled weather will then continue \ninto Saturday as the upstream low slowly nudges inland. By Sunday,\na southern stream trough associated with more abundant moisture \nwill phase with this low, bringing more significant precipitation \nto the desert southwest. However, latest model runs are trending a\nbit farther south with this southern stream system, suggesting \nthe bulk of the heavier precipitation may remain over Arizona. \nWhile fairly widespread precipitation can still be expected over \nUtah on Sunday, there remains quite a bit of uncertainty regarding\nprecipitation amounts. For example, the latest NBM 25th through \n75th percentile amounts between 12z Sunday and 12z Monday range \nfrom around 0.55 to 2.2 inches in the southern mountains and from \naround 0.4 to 1.8 inches in the southwestern valleys. If the \nhigher amounts verify, there will be an increased potential for \nflash flooding.\n\nCooler air arriving with the storm system on Sunday will bring\nlowering snow levels to the area. By the tail end of the\nprecipitation, H7 temperatures are progged to fall to near -7C\nacross northern Utah and around -2C across southern Utah.\nAccumulating mountain snow will be likely, with amounts mirroring\nthe uncertainty in the QPF. However, a general 3-6 inches with\nlocally higher amounts in the upper Cottonwoods, high Uintas, and\nnear Brian Head seem reasonable at this time. Precipitation is \nexpected to wind down by Monday afternoon. However, both the \nlatest deterministic GFS and EC bring a trailing vort lobe into \nUtah which is expected to be accompanied by a cool but drier \ncontinental airmass through the early part of next week.",
